   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of Member States to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Eswatini undertook to identify and broadly measure skills matches in the Eswatini labour market that culminated in the production of a Qualifications, Skills, and Skills Mismatch of Eswatini report in June 2023 which was officially adopted by the Ministry of Labour and Social Security in November 2023. The Qualifications, Skills and Skills Mismatch report is the first comprehensive report to be produced in the Kingdom of Eswatini and the first report to be produced in Africa. The data used to produce the report was taken from the 2021 Labour Force Survey which included a module on measuring skills mismatch. The analysis in the skills mismatch report includes a focus on the distribution of the employed population that is matched and mismatched by the type of skills, and level of education disaggregated by gender. The report has been designed to inform decision makers on policy formulation, monitoring and evaluation pertaining to the status on skills and skills mismatch in the country.             
In November 2022 the Government of Eswatini in collaboration with the Social Partners applied the Skills for Trade and Economic Diversification (STED) Anticipation Methodology of the ILO to develop a Horticulture Sector Skills Strategy that was adopted by the Ministry of Labour &amp; Social Security in November 2023. The sector assessment defined the growth objectives of the horticulture sector and identified future skills needs of the sector which were used to inform the drafting of the horticulture sector skills strategy. To support the preparation of the Labour Force Survey by the Government of Eswatini, in 2020- 2021, the ILO shared a skills mismatch module that was adapted and administered as a component of the labour force survey to collect skills mismatch data. The ILO supported the enrolment of Skills development practitioners in an E-learning course facilitated by the International Training Centre in Turin on measuring skills mismatches from Feb&#x2013;March 2022.  Subsequent to this, the ILO facilitated a 4-day training workshop in November 2022 for Labour Statisticians and skills practitioners on how to analyse skills mismatch data using the data derived from the skills mismatch survey that was integrated into the Labour Force Survey. Further to this training, the ILO maintained constant and on-going technical backstopping support to an exercise undertaken by the trained Government officials to analyse the skills mismatch data and to draft a national skills mismatch report.
The ILO facilitated a training of trainers in April 2022 of a tripartite plus team on how to apply the skills for trade and economic diversification anticipation methodology (STED.  From March &#x2013; September 2022, the ILO supported field research to assess the horticulture sector using the STED methodology.  In November 2022 the ILO in collaboration with the trained STED trainers facilitated a STED Foresight Workshop for the Horticulture Stakeholders in which the field research data was presented and validated. This workshop also began the process for the drafting of the Horticulture Sector Skills Strategy.</narrative>
   </description>
